I. The vHPU Architecture (Top-Down Software)
To emulate physical geometry on binary silicon, we must redefine how memory and processing interact.
The Hyper-Heap (Memory): Instead of a linear array of addresses (RAM), the vHPU memory is a sparse adjacency tensor. It stores nodes and their N-arity bonds (\Xi^{\langle N \rangle}).
The Rulial ISA (Instruction Set): The emulator processes a specialized assembly language of topological operations rather than arithmetic ones:
BOND (v1, v2, ..., vN): Allocates a new hyperedge.
CLEAVE (Xi_ID): Breaks a bond, representing a cascade fragmentation.
CONTRACT (Xi_ID, Psi_ID): Fuses two bonds over shared nodes.
INVERT (Xi_ID): Applies the T-dual bounce R_{\mathrm{eff}} = \max(r, \alpha'/r), locking the dimension.
Virtual Thermodynamics (The Bottleneck Metric): Because we are emulating topology on binary silicon, a single CONTRACT operation might require thousands of CPU clock cycles (searching arrays, updating pointers). The vHPU will track this "Virtual Heat" to prove exactly why the physical macroscopic experiments (which do this with zero algorithmic overhead) are necessary.
